# pi-gen's own configuration for wiretap-appliance.
#
# Chassis-owned and rewritten by every render. Put a deploy target or a shell
# account in `config.local` beside this file, which is gitignored and appended
# after this one, so it wins.

IMG_NAME='wiretap-appliance'
RELEASE='trixie'

# **stage2 and no further.** stage3 upward is a desktop, and an appliance that
# ships a window manager it never draws on is a bigger card, a longer build and
# more to patch. The chassis's own stage is last, and `build-image.sh` puts a
# `SKIP_IMAGES` marker in stage2 — it carries an `EXPORT_IMAGE` of its own, so
# without that pi-gen writes two images and only one of them is the appliance.
STAGE_LIST='stage0 stage1 stage2 stage-appliance'

# The name every card gets before it renames itself. **This is not the name the
# box answers to**: `00-appliance` installs a first-boot unit that appends the
# board's serial, because a fleet whose members are all called
# `wiretap` cannot be told apart on one network.
TARGET_HOSTNAME='wiretap'

LOCALE_DEFAULT='en_AU.UTF-8'
TIMEZONE_DEFAULT='Australia/Sydney'
WPA_COUNTRY='AU'
KEYBOARD_KEYMAP='us'
KEYBOARD_LAYOUT='English (US)'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shell account, which is pi-gen's decision more than ours
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **pi-gen always creates a first user**, defaulting to `pi`; there is no
# setting that means "no account at all". What is controllable is whether it has
# a password, and this ships none: `stage1` always creates the account with
# `--disabled-login`, and only runs `chpasswd` afterwards when `FIRST_USER_PASS`
# is set. Leaving it unset is what stops a card leaving with a credential every
# other card also has.
#
# **`DISABLE_FIRST_BOOT_USER_RENAME` is deliberately not set here.** Setting it
# to 1 makes `build.sh` refuse to start unless `FIRST_USER_PASS` is also set —
# it says so and exits 1 — so the two are one decision and both belong to the
# consumer. Left unset, the account-setup wizard it controls would be installed
# by `export-image`; `build-image.sh` skips that substage instead, and says why.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The way in, if there is one
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rendered from `[targets.image]` in `appliance.toml` — `root_ssh_key`,
# `first_user`, `first_user_ssh_key`, `passwordless_sudo` — and a key and never
# a password.
# With none of them set this is `ENABLE_SSH=0` and a card has no way in but a
# console, which is the shape a shipped appliance wants. A developer's own key
# goes in `config.local`, spelled the same way, and wins because it is sourced
# last.
#
# `PUBKEY_SSH_ROOT` is `00-appliance`'s rather than pi-gen's: sshd's Debian
# default admits root by key and nothing else, so it needs no account, no shell
# and no sudo. It is declared for export because pi-gen runs each stage script
# as a child process, and a name `config.local` merely assigned would never
# reach it. The first-user form is pi-gen's own, except that `00-appliance`
# gives that user the login shell pi-gen grants only alongside a password.
ENABLE_SSH=0
export PUBKEY_SSH_ROOT

DEPLOY_COMPRESSION='xz'
